**Diary — 2026-06-14 — Porting the CUDA engine to HIP on gfx906**

Today I moved the simulation engine from CUDA to HIP on a Vega-class GPU, target `gfx906`. The code compiled, ran, and produced bit-identical results for the small regression suite, but the port was not a simple find-and-replace. The engine is a particle/field solver with a hot kernel that iterates over particles, accumulates forces, and writes back positions and velocities.

The first change was the toolchain. I stopped using `nvcc` and switched to `hipcc`:

```bash
hipcc -O3 --offload-arch=gfx906 -o engine_hip engine_hip.cpp
```

The CUDA-style kernel launch syntax mostly worked:

```cpp
__global__ void integrate_particles(Particle* p, int n, float dt)
{
    int i = blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x;
    if (i >= n) return;

    float fx = 0.0f;
    float fy = 0.0f;
    float fz = 0.0f;

    for (int j = 0; j < n; ++j) {
        float dx = p[i].x - p[j].x;
        float dy = p[i].y - p[j].y;
        float dz = p[i].z - p[j].z;
        float r2 = dx*dx + dy*dy + dz*dz + 1e-6f;
        float inv_r = rsqrtf(r2);
        float inv_r3 = inv_r * inv_r * inv_r;
        float s = 1.0f / r2;
        fx += dx * s;
        fy += dy * s;
        fz += dz * s;
    }

    p[i].vx += fx * dt;
    p[i].vy += fy * dt;
    p[i].vz += fz * dt;
    p[i].x  += p[i].vx * dt;
    p[i].y  += p[i].vy * dt;
    p[i].z  += p[i].vz * dt;
}
